<section id="story">
<h2>Brand story</h2>
<p>Eclipse is W.W. Wood Products' frameless full-access answer to the demand for <strong>European-style cabinetry at a domestic price point</strong>. Officially branded "eclipse by Shiloh," it's built in the same Dudley, Missouri factory as Shiloh, by the same craftspeople, with the same hardware program — but engineered around a contemporary frameless box rather than a face-frame.</p>

<p>For a designer who's been told "frameless means Europe and a $200K minimum," Eclipse is a meaningful counter-narrative: American-made frameless, ships in six weeks, on the same WW Wood delivery as a Shiloh order in the same house. And it's the <em>only</em> WW Wood brand that offers the specialty-materials library — high-gloss acrylic, matte velvet acrylic, gloss HPL, matte noir HPL (RAUVISIO), wood-grain HPL, TFL, reconstituted oak and walnut veneers, and a new PV polymer laminate.</p>

<p>Market position: <strong>modern semi-custom</strong>, between domestic face-frame premium brands and European frameless brands (Pronorm, SieMatic, Poggenpohl). The sweet spot is a contemporary kitchen where the homeowner wants European look and European materials without European cost or lead time.</p>

<div class="talking-point">
  <strong>Talking point for the designer:</strong> "Eclipse is what you reach for when your client wants frameless and wants matte acrylic or HPL fronts but doesn't want to pay for German engineering or wait three months. Same factory as Shiloh, same Blum hardware, same finishing operation — different box geometry, and a materials library that Shiloh doesn't offer."
</div>
</section>

<section id="construction">
<h2>Construction &amp; specs anatomy</h2>
<p>The big question designers ask about a frameless line is "is this a real full-access box, or a face-frame box without the frame?" The answer for Eclipse is real full-access — built around frameless geometry from the ground up.</p>

<div class="anatomy-wrap">
<table class="anatomy">
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Construction</th><td>Frameless full-access — no face frame. Doors and drawers fully overlay the box, giving the clean continuous front a contemporary kitchen requires.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Cabinet box</th><td>3/4" particleboard standard. <strong>Plywood upgrade (+10%)</strong> available in most species — plywood core with MDF outer bands for stability; some materials/finishes not compatible with plywood.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Box panels</th><td>3/4" thick top, bottom, side panels; 3/4" thick adjustable shelving; 3/4" thick hanging rail; 3/4" × 4" stretchers for stability and rigidity.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Edge banding</th><td>PVC edge banding, color-coordinated to the door material (not always an exact match — designers should set expectations on this).</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Interior color</th><td>Choice of maple print, white, or linen laminate interior (some restrictions by product).</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Drawer box options</th><td>5/8" wood dovetail (standard, $0), 5/8" simulated metal drawerbox (standard, $0), 3/4" wood dovetail with branded Eclipse logo (+$57, or $107 in walnut), Blum LEGRABOX stainless steel (+$372 — full extension included).</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Drawer guides</th><td>Blum TANDEM edge 7/8 extension with BLUMOTION soft-close standard ($0). Full extension (FEG) +$72 — LEGRABOX includes full extension.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Door hinges</th><td>Blum CLIP top BLUMOTION soft-close, easy adjustment, concealed.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Interior access</th><td>Up to 10% wider drawer and door openings vs. face-frame at the same outside dimension — that's the storage win a designer can actually measure.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Sizing</th><td>Square cabinet mods up to 6" in H/W/D = 30%; over 6" = 50%; angled mods = 50%. Free width reductions up to 30% of an order. Free depth reductions at 13", 15", 16", 18", 21", 24" base.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Wall cabinet tops/bottoms</th><td>Standard finished tops and bottoms on wall cabinets (with some product-specific restrictions).</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Open shelving</th><td>Pairs cleanly with the new 1¼" edge-banded plywood floating shelf for open display sections. <a href="/playbook/shiloh-edge-banded-shelf">See spec details</a> — currently spec'd through Shiloh SKUs.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Showroom merchandising</th><td>Eclipse Merchandiser Display (ECL-M-DISP2 / model 5570) — 64" W × 84" H × 24" D, holds 44 sample doors. <a href="/playbook/eclipse-merchandiser-display">See display spec card</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Color combinations</th><td>600+ possible material × color combinations across paints, stains, laminates, acrylics, and reconstituted veneers.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Lead time</th><td>~6 weeks from order acknowledgment — same cycle as Shiloh and Aspect.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Warranty</th><td>10-year limited warranty.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Made in</th><td>Dudley, Missouri — W.W. Wood Products.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="talking-point">
  <strong>Talking point for the designer:</strong> "The frameless storage advantage isn't theoretical — up to 10% wider drawers and openings at the same outside dimension. And if the designer cares about the box being plywood (some do), it's a 10% upcharge — Eclipse defaults to 3/4" particleboard, which is actually the European standard for frameless because it's dimensionally more stable than plywood at that size."
</div>
</section>

<section id="door-styles">
<h2>Door styles</h2>
<p>Eclipse offers <strong>the same 50+ door styles as Shiloh</strong> (Hanover, Square Flat Panel, Square Raised Panel, Aspen, Heritage, Lancaster, the full Group A and Group B lineup) plus one Eclipse-exclusive: <strong>Metropolitan</strong>, the modern slab door that's the platform for the specialty materials.</p>

<p>The pricing groups carry over from Shiloh: Group A (no upcharge), Group B (+$44/door). The Metropolitan slab is Group A.</p>

<h3>What's special about Metropolitan</h3>
<p>Metropolitan is the only door style that can be ordered in the specialty material library — high-gloss acrylic, matte velvet acrylic, gloss HPL, matte noir HPL (RAUVISIO), wood-grain HPL, TFL, and the reconstituted oak/walnut veneers. Other door styles run on the standard wood species in paints or stains.</p>

<p>Edge options on Metropolitan in specialty materials: matching, satin aluminum-look, or brushed aluminum-look. Acrylic versions can also be specified with a 3D edge.</p>

<div class="talking-point">
  <strong>Talking point for the designer:</strong> "If a designer wants Hanover face-frame look in Eclipse, they can have it — the door library is the same as Shiloh. But the line really exists for Metropolitan: that's the door that opens up the matte acrylic, the HPL, the TFL, and the PV. If a client wants European matte fronts at a fraction of European pricing, that's the path."
</div>
</section>

<section id="finishes">
<h2>Finishes &amp; color program</h2>
<p>Eclipse pulls from the same finishing operation as Shiloh and adds an entire specialty-materials library that Shiloh doesn't offer. Four categories to walk a designer through:</p>

<div class="finish-grid">
  <div class="finish-card">
    <h3>Paints</h3>
    <p>Full Shiloh paint program: 25 standard Sherwin-Williams colors (10% upcharge) plus the entire Sherwin-Williams palette via custom paint match (20% upcharge, $750 setup fee on orders under $15,000 list). 2025 Trend Colors apply: Shoji White, Quietude, Rookwood Shutter Green.</p>
    <p class="small">Olde World finish (40% upcharge) also available on Eclipse paint program. Glazing is paint-program compatible on stains only.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="finish-card">
    <h3>Stains &amp; real-wood</h3>
    <p>Stains across the same species library as Shiloh — Maple, Alder, Cherry, Red Oak, Hickory, Walnut, Select Poplar, American Poplar, plain/quarter/rift-cut White Oak, and rustic variants. Plus <strong>reconstituted veneers</strong>: Reconstituted White Oak and Reconstituted Walnut (4% upcharge) — engineered veneers with consistent grain that designers reach for on long runs.</p>
    <p class="small">Stain matching across a Shiloh + Eclipse split kitchen is possible because the finishing line is the same.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="finish-card">
    <h3>Acrylic, HPL &amp; TFL (Metropolitan only)</h3>
    <p><strong>High-gloss acrylic</strong> and <strong>matte velvet acrylic</strong> — color-matched PMMA top layer with zero-joint edge-banding. <strong>Gloss HPL</strong>, <strong>matte noir HPL (RAUVISIO)</strong>, and <strong>wood-grain HPL</strong> (4% deduct each). <strong>TFL</strong> (Thermally Fused Laminate, 25% deduct) — textured to simulate wood grain, horizontal or vertical orientation.</p>
    <p class="small">All of these specialty materials are <strong>Metropolitan door style only</strong>. RAUVISIO Noir is anti-microbial, ultra-low VOC, phenol-free, and resists scratches and fingerprints.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="finish-grid" style="grid-template-columns: 1fr; margin-top:1.25rem;">
  <div class="finish-card">
    <h3>PV (Polymer Laminate) — NEW</h3>
    <p>Eclipse's newest material: <strong>PV polymer laminate</strong>, 10% deduct off base. A durable, contemporary surface that drops price compared to acrylic or HPL while staying in the modern aesthetic. Like the other laminates, available on Metropolitan only.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="talking-point">
  <strong>Talking point for the designer:</strong> "Here's the price ladder if the client wants the modern look. Cheapest to most expensive on a slab front: TFL (25% deduct), PV (10% deduct), HPL (4% deduct), painted wood (10% upcharge for standard paint or 20% for custom), matte or gloss acrylic (no upcharge or deduct — they sit right at base). All of them on Metropolitan, all of them on the same Eclipse box, all shipping in six weeks."
</div>
</section>

<section id="specify">
<h2>When to specify Eclipse</h2>
<p>The simple decision rule:</p>

<div class="rule-card">
<p><strong>Reach for Eclipse when:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>The aesthetic is contemporary, modern, or Euro-leaning — slab, narrow shaker, textured</li>
  <li>The client wants frameless full-access storage but isn't ready for European pricing or lead times</li>
  <li>The project mixes a primary kitchen and a basement bar / butler's pantry / ADU and you want continuity at a lower price tier</li>
  <li>The showroom needs a modern story to anchor the contemporary side of the floor — pair with the Eclipse Merchandiser Display</li>
  <li>The designer wants PV or laminate finishes in a US-made line</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Reach for a different Pinnacle brand when:</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>The project is traditional, transitional, or needs inset / beaded inset — <a href="/dealer-hub/product-deep-dives/shiloh">Shiloh</a> fits better</li>
  <li>The client is buying into German fitted kitchen as a whole-house design language with handleless integrated profiles — Pronorm is the right answer</li>
  <li>The job is value-driven with a tight lead — <a href="/dealer-hub/product-deep-dives/aspect">Aspect</a> serves that price point</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

